// One rule specialized twice by `with`, which is what a grammar reading several dialects writes.
//
// `parse … with (Comma = …) as Name` clones the call graph reached from `List` with one rule put in
// another's place (docs/syntax.md §5.1), and each clone is a reader of its own in the generated
// file. Until this file no snapshot held a `with`, so what the cloning emits — how many copies of
// `More` and `Item` there are, what each is called, which `#line` each construction points back to —
// could change without a line of any diff.
//
// Three publications of `List`: as written, with `;` in the place of `,`, and with an operand
// in its place, a choice, which becomes a rule of its own declared where the `with` is written.
// `More`'s construction is written once and runs in three readers; every copy of it in
// Specialized.gram.g.cs should point back to the one line below.

Item : @int = text: ['0'..'9']+ => @(int.Parse(text))

Comma = ','

More : @int = Comma & value: Item => @(value)

List : @int = first: Item & rest: More* => @(first + rest.Length)

parse List
parse List with (Comma = ';') as ParseSemicolons
parse List with (Comma = (',' | ';')) as ParseLoose
